  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nI'll design a population-based optimization strategy combined with multi-resolution analysis to better navigate the complex non-convex landscape of the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. The core idea is to use evolutionary techniques to explore promising regions of the optimization surface and exploit gradient information from local optimizations to refine solutions. This differs substantially from the single-solution trajectory used in the parent implementation.\n\nThe non-convex nature of the problem often traps local optimization methods like L-BFGS-B in poor solutions. By maintaining a population of candidate solutions and strategically transferring information between resolutions, we can overcome these limitations. The key innovation is using a history-based repair method that preserves significant features when changing resolutions, along with a restart mechanism that combines parent solutions with new initializations.\n\nThis approach addresses three bottleneck areas:\n1. Difficulty escaping shallow local minima\n2. Inefficient parameter tuning across resolutions\n3. Challenge of maintaining valid representations during adaptive resolution changes",
